.TH GIT-@-SAVE 1 "August 2025" "GitAT v1.1.0" "Git Workflow Management Tool"
.SH NAME
git-@-save \- Securely save changes with validation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git @ save
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIMESSAGE\fR]
.br
.B git @ save
\fB-m\fR \fIMESSAGE\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \fBsave\fR command securely saves current work with a commit message. It provides validation and follows best practices for committing changes.
.PP
If no commit message is provided, the command will prompt for one with an interactive form. The command automatically stages all changes before committing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -m, --message
Specify commit message directly
.TP
.B -h, --help
Show help message
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.B MESSAGE
Commit message (optional, will prompt if not provided). If provided, it should follow Conventional Commits specification.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Save changes with a message:
.RS
.nf
git @ save "Add user authentication system"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Save changes with the -m flag:
.RS
.nf
git @ save -m "Fix login validation error"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Save changes interactively (will prompt for message):
.RS
.nf
git @ save
.fi
.RE
.SH WORKFLOW
.PP
1. Validates that we're in a Git repository
.br
2. Checks for uncommitted changes
.br
3. Stages all changes (git add -A)
.br
4. Creates a commit with the provided or entered message
.br
5. Shows success message with branch information
.SH VALIDATION
.PP
The command performs several validation checks:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Ensures we're in a Git repository
.IP \(bu 2
Verifies there are changes to commit
.IP \(bu 2
Validates commit message format (when provided)
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
.BR git-@ (1),
.BR git-@-work (1),
.BR git-@-squash (1)